WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:07.114
Hi! We introduce VI-CuRL, our NeurIPS
2026 paper on verifier-independent

00:07.134 --> 00:09.547
reinforcement learning for reasoning.

00:10.300 --> 00:14.374
Reasoning models like DeepSeek-R1
learn from verifiable rewards:

00:14.394 --> 00:18.277
a verifier checks each answer,
and correct ones are rewarded.

00:18.600 --> 00:22.770
But good verifiers are costly, and
for open-ended tasks they don't exist.

00:22.790 --> 00:24.205
Can we drop the verifier?

00:24.933 --> 00:29.475
Verifier-free methods reward the model
with its own signals instead, such as

00:29.495 --> 00:31.215
majority vote or low entropy.

00:31.567 --> 00:34.616
But these rewards are noisy,
so the gradients are noisy.

00:34.636 --> 00:37.739
The variance becomes destructive,
and training collapses.

00:38.067 --> 00:46.319
On Qwen2.5-Math 1.5B, TTRL drops to 0.1
percent accuracy, and RENT to 4.8, far below

00:46.339 --> 00:48.485
the base model's 20.3.

00:49.200 --> 00:53.267
Our idea comes from how people learn: start
with what you are sure about, then take

00:53.287 --> 00:54.203
on everything else.

00:54.533 --> 00:59.292
Existing curricula need a verifier. We
use the model's own confidence instead:

00:59.312 --> 01:01.681
one minus its normalized token entropy.

01:02.033 --> 01:05.983
Confidence is never a reward. It only
decides which prompts we train on.

01:06.700 --> 01:11.737
In each GRPO step, we generate responses
for a batch of prompts, with rewards from

01:11.757 --> 01:14.636
any source, and rank the
prompts by confidence.

01:14.967 --> 01:20.013
Only the top fraction, beta t, enters the
update, re-weighted by one over beta t.

01:20.367 --> 01:23.523
Beta is annealed from 20
percent up to the full batch:

01:23.543 --> 01:26.641
first a stable, low-variance
subset, then everything.

01:27.367 --> 01:32.473
Is this biased? Theorem 1 bounds the gap
to the true objective by two L max, times

01:32.493 --> 01:33.536
one minus beta t.

01:33.867 --> 01:38.286
So the bias vanishes as beta t reaches
one, even if the confidence is not

01:38.306 --> 01:38.955
calibrated.

01:39.667 --> 01:44.728
Theorem 2 splits the gradient variance
into action, problem, and masking terms;

01:44.748 --> 01:47.237
masking vanishes as beta t goes to one.

01:47.567 --> 01:51.751
Confident prompts reduce the first two,
so updates point in a more consistent

01:51.771 --> 01:52.297
direction.

01:53.033 --> 01:58.611
In practice, early in training, the kept
subset has 20 to 45 percent lower variance,

01:58.631 --> 02:01.476
and the ratio returns to one as beta grows.

02:02.200 --> 02:09.266
On six math benchmarks, verifier-free
VI-CuRL lifts Qwen2.5-Math 1.5B to 34.2

02:09.286 --> 02:13.868
percent with majority vote,
and 31.7 with entropy.

02:14.200 --> 02:21.048
On DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen 1.5B, verifier-free
VI-CuRL reaches 58.6, above training

02:21.068 --> 02:24.073
with a ground-truth verifier at 50.8.

02:24.400 --> 02:28.784
With a verifier, it is best on all four
models, and it also stays stable on

02:28.804 --> 02:30.487
general-knowledge benchmarks.

02:31.200 --> 02:36.907
Both parts matter. Random or least-confident
subsets give little or no gain;

02:36.927 --> 02:40.222
confidence lifts accuracy from 47.4 to 56.0.

02:40.567 --> 02:44.159
And without annealing, a static
filter collapses later in training.

02:44.867 --> 02:49.284
To sum up: VI-CuRL turns confidence
into an annealed curriculum that is

02:49.304 --> 02:53.346
asymptotically unbiased, reduces
variance, and prevents collapse.

02:53.667 --> 02:55.857
Our code is on GitHub. Thanks for watching!
